            {
              "id": "ascham2020e1-q14e-ii",
              "type": "review",
              "difficulty": "hard",
              "timeLimit": 60,
              "stem": "Hence sketch the graph of \\(y=\\frac{1}{|x^2-2x-4|}\\)",
              "hint": "Reflect the negative part of y = x² − 2x − 4 up, then take reciprocals: zeros → asymptotes.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "First take the absolute value: the part of the parabola below the \\(x\\)-axis (between \\(1\\pm\\sqrt5\\)) is reflected up, so the vertex becomes \\((1,5)\\).",
                  "work": "\\(|x^2-2x-4|\\ge0,\\qquad |f(1)|=5\\)"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Now take the reciprocal. Zeros of \\(f\\) become vertical asymptotes; large values become small (\\(y\\to0\\) as \\(x\\to\\pm\\infty\\)); the maximum 5 in the middle becomes a minimum \\(\\frac15\\).",
                  "work": "\\(x=1\\pm\\sqrt5\\ \\text{(vertical asymptotes)},\\qquad\\left(1,\\tfrac15\\right),\\qquad y\\text{-int }\\tfrac14\\)"
                },
